  "supplier_reading": {
    "statement_quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
    "hypotheses_checked": "For each fixed beta, Y_j integrable by unchanged model and c_j constant/integrable under probability law. Apply two-variable linearity on that one probability space, obtaining integrable difference and E_beta[Y_j-c_j]=E_beta[Y_j]-c_j=0. No assumption about simultaneous realization/measurability over parameter space; no Gaussianity, covariance or independence required."
  },
